 { In this opening section, we're going to cover a selection of examples designed to help us to accurately evaluate spatial control in chess. In each position, we will try to judge whether greater space control translates into an objective advantage or whether it is only illusory. Let's start with a tricky example, where each side has a claim to the space advantage. Before reading on, feel free to stop and consider which side you think stands better and why. } 1. { -KEY- } Nd4 $1 { If you evaluated White as standing better, you are correct. Let's explore why this is: (1) White has the c4/d5 chain while Black has the f5/e4 chain. In terms of square control both sides are roughly even, but White's chain can be supported with b3 while Black's cannot. (2) Black has the d3 outpost, but it is well-guarded by White. On the other hand, the outpost on e6 is less secure. (3) Black has a weak square on h5, that White is ready to target with their queen. (4) White's pieces are more central and are not stepping on each other's toes like Black's. (5) White can break the e4/f5 structure with g4 at the right moment. Black's only pawn breaks are ...c6 or ...b5, but White has these squares under control. There is a lot to digest with the above. Do not worry about keeping a long list of factors in your mind during your games; over time the analysis of many games will allow you to develop your intuition organically. Let's now see how the play might continue: } Qe7 { Black tries a quiet developing queen move. } ( 1... Nb6 $6 { Black might try to pressure the pawn chain, but after } 2. b3 $16 { White easily supports their strong pawn chain, while Black's knight on b6 is left completely out of the game. } ) 
2. Qe2 $1 { Connecting the rooks and preparing to bring the only inactive piece into the game with e.g. Rae1 on the next move. Black's queenside pieces remain passive and without good prospects. } Nf6 { Black could not sit with the knight on d7 forever, so they bring it to the most active square. } 3. g4 $1 { A well-timed pawn break! } fxg4 4. f5 $1 { If Black's knight had still been on d7 this plan would not have worked since then Black could have played ...Ne5, targeting the outposts on d3 and f3. } Nd3 5. Ne6 $1 { White ignores the invader and establishes their own knight on the power outpost on e6. } ( 5. Bxd3 $2 { White should not take the bait, since after } exd3 6. Qxd3 Nh5 { Black's position becomes too active, while } 7. hxg4 { does not work because of } Qh4 $1 { when after } 8. gxh5 { Black has a draw with } Qg3 $11 ) 
Bxe6 6. dxe6 $1 { Keeping the f-file closed, and setting up a possible Nc3-d5 knight jump in the future. } Ne5 { Black's best bet is to centralise their knight, admitting that the d3 outpost was not so strong after all. This can be contrasted with White's play on the e6 outpost, which has yielded them a very strong protected passed pawn. } ( 6... Nxb2 $2 { The greedy pawn capture would be a costly loss of time, and after } 7. Nxe4 Nxe4 8. Bxe4 $18 { the Black knight is stranded on b2, while White's connected passed pawns on e6 and f5 will combine with the bishop pair to create unstoppable threats on the kingside. } ) 
7. Kh1 { The position remains complex, but with pressure against e4 and g4 it is very easy for Black to go wrong. } gxh3 $2 { Black grabs a pawn, but now the open g-file will prove decisive. } 8. Rg1 Kh8 9. Nxe4 Nxe4 10. Bxe4 Rf6 { Black tries to hold things together, but the connected passed pawns on f5 and e6 are too strong. } 11. Rg3 Rg8 12. Rag1 Qe8 13. Rxh3 $18 { Black has no defense against the continued assault along the g- and h-files. This example has been a very tricky one, and a tough start to this important chapter! Fortunately, most of the time there is a useful rule of thumb that will help you to judge when space is important or not. The rest of the examples in this section will discuss this rule. } 
