 { We ended the last example by saying that there is an important rule that will help you quickly judge whether space is a big advantage or not, across most positions. The general rule that is important to know states that 'space becomes less valuable as minor pieces are exchanged'. Put another way, the more minor pieces on the board the better it is for the side with more space. Before we show a few examples of this in action, I should say that it is best to treat this as a guideline and not an absolute rule since many exceptions exist. Overall however, it is a helpful rule of thumb. Take a moment to consider whether you think White's space advantage is significant here or not. } 1. { -KEY- } Ra6 $1 { The White advantage is in fact winning here. The problem is that only one set of minor pieces have been exchanged. The reason why minor pieces on the board tends to hurt the side with less space is that they will often be left without good squares to go to. Here, Black's knights are both paralysed while their bishop sits passively on g7. } Rd7 { Defending the pawn on a7. } 2. Bc3 { Controlling the d2 square, preparing to bring the knight into play. } Rc7 { Overprotecting c6, hoping that this will allow them to redirect the knight on e7 to a more productive role elsewhere. } 3. Nd2 { Unlike Black, White's minor pieces are not struggling for squares. Either the knight or the bishop can go to c4 next. } g5 { This highlights a common problem of lacking space. Black needs to activate their knights, but in trying to do so they create new weaknesses in their position. } 4. Bc4 Qd7 5. Nf1 $1 { The White knight eyes up the newly gained outpost on f5. } Ng6 6. Ne3 Nce7 { Black has managed to two-step with their knights, but even after this effort, the e7 knight remains passively locked down by White's structure. } 7. Rd1 Qc8 8. Rxa7 $18 { White converts their positional advantage into an extra pawn, while still maintaining much superior piece configuration than their opponent. The reason for Black's quick collapse was not the weakness of their queenside pawns, but rather the lack of mobility of their minor pieces. We'll keep the same structure for our next example, but this time without so many minor pieces. } 
