 { Here we have the same structure as in the previous example, but without any knights on the board. By removing the problem pieces, the position has gone from losing for Black to a comfortable equality. Let's take a look at how play might unfold. } 1. { -KEY- } Ra6 { White begins by targeting the c6 pawn, as in the previous example. } h5 { Black prepares a clever tactical idea to indirectly defend against the pressure on c6. } 2. Qa4 Kh7 $1 ( 2... Rdc7 { should also be fine, but a little more passive than the text move. } ) 
3. Rxc6 { White has no way of improving the position further so they grab the pawn, but Black has a clever reply in store. } ( 3. Bc3 $6 Qc4 $13 { White cannot allow an invasion of the Black queen. Note how this was not possible in our previous example as White's bishop on f1 kept the light squares well-guarded throughout. } ) 
Rxb4 $1 { The White queen is overloaded. } 4. Qxb4 Qxc6 $11 { The position has simplified, and White's 'space advantage' has fully disintegrated. Black was able to deal with the pressure with ease since they did not have to contend with passive knights, their Achilles heel in the previous position. Let's show one more set of examples with the same structure but different piece arrangements. } 
