 { In this first position, taken from an online game between Magnus Carlsen and GM Bortynk, White stands much better due to his space advantage and the presence of all eight minor pieces. } 1. { -KEY- } b3 $1 { A strong move, robbing Black of any hope to trade minor pieces with 1...Nc4. } Bf8 { A quiet move would not have saved Black. Play might have continued: } ( 1... h4 $2 { In the game, Black tried to make progress on the kingside, but quickly got steamrolled after } 2. f4 $1 Ned7 3. g4 $1 { Securing even more space. Black is already lost since the only target that he can aim at is the e4 pawn, but after } Nc5 4. Bf2 $18 { White protects the central pawn, while taking aim at Black's stranded pawn on h4. White will be up material soon, with the space advantage still intact and all of Black's minor pieces struggling for activity. } ) 
2. f4 $1 Ned7 { Black's knights are stepping on each other's toes, while the light-squared bishop has no prospects. } 3. Qf2 $1 { Improving the position and getting ready to slowly build up the pressure on the kingside. } Bg7 { As frequently happens in positions with a lack of space, Black is forced to shuffle his pieces back and forth. } ( 3... Nb6 $2 { One of the ideas behind 3. Qf2 is to take away this forward jump from the Black knight. } 4. Ndb5 $1 cxb5 5. Nxb5 $18 { White will recover their knight next and emerge at least a pawn to the good. } ) 
4. Re2 { Preparing to increase the pressure along the d-file. } Nf8 5. Red2 Bd7 6. Rd3 Qc8 7. Kh2 $1 { White continues to keep everything safe. When playing with the space advantage, it is usually a good idea to not rush. Instead, you want to patiently improve the position and starve the opponent of counterplay. Such a way of playing has been compared to the methods of a python snake, gradually constricting its prey. } Rb8 { Black continues to shuffle. } 8. Nf3 Nxe4 { Black has nothing better than to take the pawn, since d6 is falling. } 9. Nxe4 Rxe4 10. Rxd6 { The position suddenly opens up, with White's pieces much more actively placed. } Re8 { Stepping the rook back to avoid it getting trapped in the centre of the board. } 11. Ne5 $1 ( { There is no risk of } 11. Ne5 Bxe5 $2 { since that would leave the dark squares too weak. } ) 
Bf5 12. Bb6 { Targeting the a5 pawn. } Ra8 13. Bf1 { Tranferring the bishop to c4 to target the f7 pawn. } Be6 { Stopping the threat, but taking away an important square from the queen. } 14. Rd8 $1 Rxd8 15. Rxd8 $16 { White is close to winning with the queen for rook and bishop. Once again the problem for Black was not so much the lack of space, but the fact he could not find good squares for his pieces.Let's revisit the same structure next, but this time with less material on the board. } 
