 { Here we have removed Black's problem knight on e5, and at the same time we have introduced a second imbalance into the position: opposite-colored bishops! Black's backward pawn on d6 is no longer of concern, since the bishop is excellently placed to guard it. At the same time, f2-f4 advances lose their sting as White has to be careful to not weaken his dark squares. Let's see how the game might play out: } 1. { -KEY- } Qd4 { White finds nothing better than to centralise their queen. } Nd7 $1 { Heading to the c5 square, which functions like an outpost since b2-b4 is not possible. } 2. b3 Nc5 3. h4 { Due to the lack of a target to attack, White is forced to make quiet improving moves to their structure. } Bg7 $1 ( { A good moment to pivot back to the long diagonal, since } 3... Bg7 3. Qxd6 { would lose the knight on c3. } ) 
4. Qd2 Re6 $15 { Defending the d6 pawn and preparing to add pressure against e4. We can see that Black has not only been unbothered by having less space, but in fact has stood a little better throughout. White had no active plan, while Black has used the last few moves to reconfigure their minor pieces to better squares. } 
